  "result": "I wrote the revised record for `NRS_484B_307_9a_o2` to `record.jsonl`; nothing has run against the gate yet.\n\n**Why the gate failed:** span `b3` (\"vehicular traffic\") was written as \"is traffic and is not a pedestrian\". The run's reading leaves every vehicle class unresolved for the pedestrian definition (`DEF-NRS-484A-165::pedestrian`). So \"not a pedestrian\" was unknown for every vehicle, and the subject class could never be true.\n\n**The fix:** `b3` and the binder now use \"is traffic\" (`DEF-NRS-484A-285::traffic`) together with \"is a vehicle\" (`DEF-NRS-484A-320::vehicle`).\n- \"Vehicular\" is now read through the posted definition of \"vehicle\". That definition already leaves out pedestrians, animals, human-powered devices, electric bicycles and electric scooters.\n- This is the same mapping the sibling node `NRS_484B_307_9a_o1` gives this span, so the two nodes no longer disagree.\n- The coder found nothing wrong with the use of \"pedestrian\" in `b9` (the parties x must yield to), so it is unchanged.\n\nOne reading change to note: the old record kept cycles and scooters in as \"other conveyances\". Under the vehicle definition, human-powered devices, electric bicycles and electric scooters are now outside this rule. Motorcycles and mopeds stay in.\n\nOther changes:\n- The applicability prose now states the new class.\n- The obligation prose now repeats the full choice of intersection, as the coder's rule already does.\n- The `b11` residue now has `scope: \"none\"`.\n- The four scenarios name x as a motor vehicle.\n- I dropped the old `pedestrian` atom from `b3`.",
